      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the area of the square and the area of the four quarter circles that are tangent to the sides of the square. The shaded area will be the area of the square minus the area of the four quarter circles.\n\nFirst, let's find the side length of the square. The problem states that the square has a side length of 20 inches.\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the square} = \\text{side length}^2 = 20^2 = 400 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the area of one full circle. Since the circles are tangent to each other and to the sides of the square, the diameter of each circle is equal to the side length of the square divided by \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) (because the circles are arranged such that they touch each other and the sides of the square). However, a simpler approach is to recognize that the radius of each circle is equal to half the side length of the square. Therefore, the radius \\(r\\) of each circle is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{20}{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{20}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{4} = 5\\sqrt{2} \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area of one full circle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one full circle} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (5\\sqrt{2})^2 = \\pi \\cdot 50 = 50\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\nSince there are four quarter circles, the total area of the four quarter circles is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of four quarter circles} = 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi r^2 = \\pi r^2 = 50\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\nThe shaded area is the area of the square minus the area of the four quarter circles:\n\\[\n\\text{Shaded area} = 400 - 50\\pi\n\\]\nThus, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400 - 50\\pi}\n\\]",
